<!-- ARTICLE -->
<article class="py-16">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 blog-content">

    <p>Within 72 hours of any named storm making landfall in Tampa Bay, a wave of out-of-state contractors, fake-branded pickup trucks, and door-to-door sales reps floods Pinellas, Hillsborough, and Pasco. The Florida Attorney General's office logs thousands of storm-related contractor complaints every hurricane season, and the same three or four scam patterns account for almost all of them.</p>

    <p>Every dollar you spend on a legitimate local roofer stays in Tampa Bay. Every dollar that goes to a storm chaser leaves the state, along with any hope of warranty service, permit sign-off, or recourse when the workmanship fails 18 months later. Here is exactly how to tell them apart before you sign anything.</p>

    <h2>1. WHAT A STORM CHASER ACTUALLY IS</h2>

    <p>A storm chaser is a roofing crew, sometimes a legitimate contractor from another state, sometimes a stitched-together fake front, that follows the National Hurricane Center track and sets up in disaster zones for the FEMA and insurance payout window. The best of them are competent installers with real trucks and real crews who leave when the money dries up. The worst are 100 percent fraudulent, take deposits from 40 or 50 homeowners in a single week, and are three states away by the time the first permit gets denied.</p>

    <p>The middle group, which is the majority, sits somewhere in between. They pull real permits, they do real work, and then they disappear when the workmanship warranty claims start rolling in six months later. You are left with a leaking roof, no local recourse, and no way to enforce the manufacturer warranty because the crew was never a certified installer of the shingle they installed on your home.</p>

    <h2>2. THE 9 RED FLAGS YOU CAN SPOT IN 30 SECONDS</h2>

    <p>Any single one of these should end the conversation. Multiple together are almost diagnostic.</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Unsolicited door knock the week after a storm.</strong> Legitimate Tampa Bay roofers are booked solid post-storm and do not need to canvass. The knock itself is a filter: reputable local firms almost never door knock, and the ones that do are usually the ones you want to avoid.</li>
      <li><strong>"Free roof inspection" pitched with high urgency.</strong> Real free inspections happen on your schedule, not theirs. If the pitch is "we need to get up there right now before the next storm," they are trying to create fake damage before you can compare quotes.</li>
      <li><strong>Out-of-state license plates on the truck.</strong> Texas, Alabama, Georgia, and Oklahoma are the four most common storm-chaser home states. A Tampa Bay-based roofer has Florida plates.</li>
      <li><strong>No physical local address, or an address that is a UPS Store mailbox.</strong> Search the address on Google Maps. If it shows a shipping storefront, a virtual office, or a residential apartment, walk away. Happy Roof is at 141 Stevens Ave, Oldsmar, FL 34677 for a reason.</li>
      <li><strong>Cash-only or crypto-only payment.</strong> Real roofers accept checks, credit cards, and financing through legitimate lenders. A cash-only demand is a signal that the business does not want a paper trail.</li>
      <li><strong>Deposit demand above 30 to 50 percent, or any deposit before material delivery.</strong> Florida statute 489.126 sets rules around the 10 percent threshold, and reputable Tampa Bay contractors typically collect on delivery, not before. Full deposit up front is the classic disappear-with-the-money setup.</li>
      <li><strong>Assignment of Benefits (AOB) form.</strong> Illegal for residential roof claims signed after January 1, 2023 under SB 2A. Any door knocker still pushing an AOB is either uninformed, running an old playbook, or trying to sue your carrier on your behalf. See the AOB section below.</li>
      <li><strong>"We will pay your deductible" or "we will cover your deductible."</strong> Both are Florida insurance fraud under statute 817.234. It is a third-degree felony. Any contractor offering this is either committing fraud or setting you up to commit fraud alongside them.</li>
      <li><strong>The estimate has no line-item breakdown.</strong> A legitimate Tampa Bay roof quote itemizes tear-off, deck repair allowance, underlayment product and coverage, shingle SKU and color, ridge cap product, drip edge, valley method, ventilation upgrades, and workmanship warranty duration. A one-line "roof replacement, $12,500" is a black box.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Any two of the above together, close the door. Any four, that contact was a scam attempt full stop.</p>

    <h2>3. HOW TO VERIFY A FLORIDA ROOFING LICENSE IN 90 SECONDS</h2>

    <p>The Florida Department of Business and Professional Regulation (DBPR) maintains a free public license search at <a href="https://www.myfloridalicense.com/wl11.asp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">myfloridalicense.com/wl11.asp</a>. The workflow:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Open the DBPR search page.</li>
      <li>Set License Category to <strong>Construction Industry (CILB)</strong> or search by license number if you have one.</li>
      <li>Enter either the business name or the qualifier's individual name.</li>
      <li>Verify all three of the following: <strong>Status: Current, Active</strong>, <strong>License Type: Certified Roofing Contractor (CCC)</strong> for statewide work or <strong>Registered Roofing Contractor (RC)</strong> for county-specific, and <strong>Discipline History</strong> shows no complaints, fines, or suspensions in the last 5 years.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>What you are looking for: an active Florida license held by the actual person or company you are talking to. What disqualifies: expired, revoked, suspended, restricted, or "not on file." A legitimate Tampa Bay contractor is happy to hand you the license number before you even ask. Ours is CCC1337380 and takes 30 seconds to verify.</p>

    <p>Bonus check: cross-reference against the Better Business Bureau at <a href="https://www.bbb.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bbb.org</a> and the Florida Attorney General consumer complaint database. A pattern of unresolved complaints is a red flag even if the license is technically current.</p>

    <h2>4. WHY AOB CONTRACTS ARE DEAD IN FLORIDA (AND WHAT REPLACED THEM)</h2>

    <p>For years, storm chasers pitched Assignment of Benefits contracts as a "no out-of-pocket cost" way for homeowners to get a new roof. The homeowner signed away their insurance benefits to the contractor, the contractor billed the carrier directly, and if the carrier did not pay in full the contractor sued them under Florida statute 627.428, which required the losing insurer to pay all attorney fees.</p>

    <p>By 2022 the AOB loophole was driving industry-wide premium spikes, hundreds of thousands of frivolous lawsuits, and thousands of homeowners with contractor liens on properties after the roofer disappeared. The Florida legislature effectively killed residential AOBs in a special session in December 2022 with Senate Bill 2A, which:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Made AOB contracts unenforceable for residential property insurance claims signed after January 1, 2023.</li>
      <li>Eliminated one-way attorney fee shifting under 627.428 for property claims.</li>
      <li>Shortened the claim-filing window from 4 years to 1 year for supplemental claims and 2 years for initial.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Bottom line for Tampa Bay: no legitimate 2026 roofer needs you to sign an AOB. Any door-knocker post-storm form that includes AOB language is either using an old contract they never updated or is running a scam knowingly. Sign your carrier's paperwork directly, hire your roofer separately, and if there is a claim dispute, work it out through your carrier's appraisal or mediation process. See our <a href="/blog/how-to-file-roof-insurance-claim">Florida roof insurance claim guide</a> for the current process.</p>

    <h2>5. WHAT A LEGITIMATE TAMPA BAY POST-STORM WORKFLOW LOOKS LIKE</h2>

    <p>Compare the door-knocker pitch above to what an actual local roofer does:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>You call us or fill out a form.</strong> Not the other way around.</li>
      <li><strong>We schedule a free inspection within 24 to 72 hours.</strong> During peak post-storm weeks it may push to 5 days. That is normal and honest.</li>
      <li><strong>The inspector shows up in a truck with a Happy Roof wrap, in a Happy Roof shirt, with a business card and a physical Oldsmar address on it.</strong> They provide their DBPR license number without being asked.</li>
      <li><strong>They document damage with photos and drone imagery.</strong> The written report is emailed to you, not printed on a clipboard and taken away.</li>
      <li><strong>If damage warrants an insurance claim, we recommend you file with your carrier directly.</strong> We meet the adjuster on site when they come out, we do not sign anything on your behalf.</li>
      <li><strong>The quote arrives with itemized line items.</strong> Shingle brand and product line (Owens Corning Duration, GAF Timberline HDZ, etc), underlayment type, deck repair allowance, ventilation package, warranty tier.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero deposit due at signing.</strong> Material deposit of 30 to 50 percent on the day the pallet arrives at your home. Balance due at final building department inspection.</li>
      <li><strong>Permit pulled in the contractor's name.</strong> Not yours. See the permit trap below.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Any deviation from this pattern is worth asking about. Not necessarily a scam, but worth an honest conversation before you commit.</p>

    <h2>6. THE PERMIT-PULLED-IN-HOMEOWNER'S-NAME TRAP</h2>

    <p>Under Florida statute 489.103, only a licensed contractor can pull a building permit for construction work over $1,000, with an exception for owner-builders. Some storm chasers exploit the owner-builder loophole by having the homeowner sign a permit application under their own name, then doing the work as an unlicensed labor subcontractor.</p>

    <p>Why this matters to you: if you are listed as the owner-builder on the permit, <strong>you</strong> are legally responsible for the work meeting code, <strong>you</strong> are liable if a worker is injured on your property, <strong>you</strong> void the shingle manufacturer's warranty because it was not installed by a certified installer, and <strong>you</strong> have no recourse when the roof leaks in 2027 because there is no licensed contractor on the paperwork to sue. Insurance carriers regularly deny future claims on owner-builder roofs.</p>

    <p>The correct answer: the permit is pulled in the contractor's name using their DBPR license number. If a contractor asks you to sign an owner-builder permit application, that is a hard stop. They are avoiding accountability, and you are absorbing all of it.</p>

    <h2>7. THE THREE MOST COMMON TAMPA BAY POST-STORM FRAUD PATTERNS</h2>

    <p>These are the three we see repeat almost verbatim after every named storm in Pinellas and Hillsborough:</p>

    <h3>The "Free Inspection" Damage Manufacturing Pattern</h3>

    <p>A crew shows up unsolicited, climbs the roof "to inspect," and comes down with photos of damage that was not there before they went up. Sometimes they cause real damage on purpose (lifted shingles, pulled nails) to justify a claim. Sometimes they photograph a neighbor's roof and claim it is yours. Defense: never let an unsolicited contractor on your roof. Ever. Your own <a href="/blog/roof-maintenance-checklist">seasonal maintenance photos</a> from before the storm are your evidence.</p>

    <h3>The "Deductible Waiver" Insurance Fraud Pattern</h3>

    <p>The contractor offers to eat your $2,500 hurricane deductible. On paper it sounds like a discount. In reality it is insurance fraud (Florida statute 817.234), a third-degree felony for both parties, and the moment your carrier catches it, your claim is denied and you face criminal exposure. Legitimate roofers never waive deductibles. They cannot legally, and any contractor who offers is either committing fraud openly or setting you up to commit it.</p>

    <h3>The "Take the Deposit and Vanish" Pattern</h3>

    <p>The oldest scam in the book. Contractor collects a 50 to 100 percent deposit at signing, pulls no permit, does no work, and is gone within 30 days. Sometimes with a real license and a real business front, which they abandon after collecting from 20 to 40 households. Defense: zero money down, deposits only on material delivery day, verify permit pulled within 30 days of contract signing at your county building department portal.</p>

    <h2>8. HOW TO PICK A LEGITIMATE TAMPA BAY ROOFER IN 15 MINUTES</h2>

    <p>The full workflow, timed:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>3 minutes: DBPR license verification.</strong> Active CCC or RC license, no complaints in 5 years.</li>
      <li><strong>2 minutes: physical address check on Google Maps.</strong> Real office or yard, not a mailbox store.</li>
      <li><strong>3 minutes: BBB and Google Reviews scan.</strong> Look for detail-rich reviews with photos, not five-star bombs from accounts with one review each.</li>
      <li><strong>2 minutes: warranty and financing questions.</strong> Ask for the workmanship warranty in writing, ask which manufacturer certifications they hold (GAF Master Elite, Owens Corning Preferred Contractor, etc), ask about their financing partner (a real one, not "we finance internally").</li>
      <li><strong>5 minutes: read our <a href="/blog/5-questions-to-ask-your-roofer">5 questions to ask any Tampa Bay roofer</a> post</strong> before the sales meeting. Use it as your interview checklist.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Any contractor who fails on any of the first four steps is off the list. The fifth step is your interview and your gut check. If something feels wrong, walk away. There are plenty of legitimate Tampa Bay roofers, including ours, who will earn your business without pressure.</p>

    <h2>9. IF YOU HAVE ALREADY SIGNED WITH A STORM CHASER</h2>

    <p>Not lost. Take these steps in order:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Do not pay another dollar.</strong> Any additional payment gives them more leverage.</li>
      <li><strong>Send written notice of cancellation within 3 business days of signing.</strong> Under Florida statute 501.031, most home solicitation contracts (including door-to-door roofing) have a 3-day right of cancellation. Send certified mail with return receipt to the address on the contract.</li>
      <li><strong>File a complaint with DBPR</strong> at myfloridalicense.com if the contractor is licensed, or with the Florida Attorney General at myfloridalegal.com if they are not.</li>
      <li><strong>Contact your insurance carrier</strong> and inform them of the situation before any AOB or claim assignment gets filed on your behalf.</li>
      <li><strong>Get a legitimate local roofer to inspect the work</strong> if any has been done. Sometimes salvageable, sometimes not. If not, document everything for your DBPR complaint.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Happy Roof serves Tampa Bay under Florida license CCC1337380, from a physical office at 141 Stevens Ave in Oldsmar, with an A+ BBB rating and every quote itemized in writing. Call <a href="tel:8135957663" class="call-tracking-number">(813) 595-7663</a> or <a href="/contact">book a free honest inspection</a> and we will tell you the truth about what your roof actually needs, storm or no storm.</p>

  </div>
</article>

